('P', '**Safety and referral (general clinical consensus):** DSED leaves children vulnerable to exploitation and abduction. Plan supervision in public and at church, school, and camp. Directly observe the child with caregivers and with unfamiliar adults (Zeanah et al., 2016); refer for pediatric, developmental, and psychiatric evaluation; and report suspected ongoing abuse or neglect.'),
('H', '3. What works: treatment and the evidence'),
('P', '**Bottom line for class:** placement in a stable, sensitive family helps, but DSED improves less readily than RAD. Treatment combines caregiver-focused work with explicit teaching and practice of social boundaries. Coercive "attachment therapies" should be avoided.'),
